Avoid redundant closure and snapshot work

Keep closed models idle until an insertion, allocation, or equality
changes their inputs. Preserve close_until condition checks and
interrupted work. Build old primary views only when the stratum
uses them.

Skip shared trees and stop semantic comparisons at the first addition.
Subtract borrowed tree ranges without allocating temporary splits.
Reuse shared subtrees in idempotent unions and retain inherited facts
between passes so propagation preserves snapshot sharing. Rebuild
inherited views after equality replacement. Include conflicting
morphism results until equality resolves them, so retained facts
cannot erase their last owned support.

Reuse old checkpoints for fully bound canonical membership probes.
Classify these uses before code generation and keep filtering indices
that bind variables, so equality collapses do not multiply matches.

Compile scheduling workloads in simultaneous and stratified modes and
measure initial closure, sparse updates, and closure without changes.
Keep construction and result checks outside the measured operation.
Count allocation traffic in a separate build to leave timings free
of allocator instrumentation.

Cover acyclic pipelines, independent components, model chains and
cycles, nested model graphs, and joins enabled by equality merges.
Execute the benchmark fixtures in integration tests as well.

Keep historical, pre-fix, and fixed measurements of the same workloads
so costs shared by both schedules and remaining overhead stay visible.
